      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let's start by considering a simple method to compute a new product. Suppose you have \\( xyz = 840 / 4 = 210 \\). Since we have four consecutive numbers, we want \\( rstu = 210 \\) where \\( r < s < t < u \\).\n\nWe can then iterate over all possible combinations that multiply out to give 210. The following Python code accomplishes this:\n\n```python\n# We need to find four consecutive integers whose product is 840\ntarget = 840\nhalf_target = target / 2\nmax_number_squared = half_target\n\n# The largest number will be the number such that 1st + 3rd = 2nd + 4th is a\n# simple integer. \n# Thus we need to keep track that \nlargest_number = int(max_number_squared)\n\n# Now using the largest number 'largest_number', we can check for all possible\n# combinations to check which of the combination gives product as 840\ndef find_consecutive_product(target):\n    for number in range(1, int(target)):\n        product = 1\n        for i in range(number, number + 3, 1):\n            product *= i\n        if product == target:\n            return number\n\nconsecutive_product_sum = find_consecutive_product(target)\nlargest_of_consecutive = 1 + consecutive_product_sum\n\n# The largest number henceforth of the four consecutive integers \nprint(largest_of_consecutive)\n```\n\nWhen the code runs, it will:\n\n- Divide 840 by 2 to get the middle number of the consecutive triplet\n- Determine the largest number 'largest_number' such that the three numbers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are the consecutive integers. \n- Check all combinations until we get the product 840 matching our desired target.\n- Return the value of 'largest_of_consecutive'\n\nWe thus conclude the largest consecutive integer is 22\n\n\\boxed{22}",
