      "text": "To solve the problem, we first need to determine the four consecutive positive integers whose product is 840. Let’s denote these four integers as \\( n-1 \\), \\( n \\), \\( n+1 \\), and \\( n+2 \\).\n\nThe product of these four integers is given by:\n\\[\n(n-1)n(n+1)(n+2) = 840\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite the product expression as:\n\\[\n(n-1)(n+1)(n+2)n^2 = 840\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the product, we get:\n\\[\n(n-1)n(n+1)(n+2) = 840\n\\]\n\nWe can split this product into two simpler products:\n\\[\n(n-1)n(n+1)(n+2) = (n-1)n(n+2)(n+1)\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\n(n-1)n(n+2)(n+1) = 840\n\\]\n\nNow we need to solve for \\( n \\) by trial and error. Let’s start by testing some values for \\( n \\).\n\nAssume \\( n = 8 \\):\n\\[\n(n-1)n(n+2)(n+1) = 8 \\times 9 \\times 10 \\times 11 = 792\n\\]\nThis is too large, so \\( n > 8 \\).\n\nAssume \\( n = 7 \\):\n\\[\n(n-1)n(n+2)(n+1) = 7 \\times 8 \\times 9 \\times 10 = 5040\n\\]\nThis is too large, so \\( n < 7 \\).\n\nAssume \\( n = 6 \\):\n\\[\n(n-1)n(n+2)(n+1) = 5 \\times 6 \\times 7 \\times 8 = 1680\n\\]\nThis is more manageable. Let’s check if there is a perfect factorization of 840.\n\nTo find the correct \\( n \\), let's test values iteratively until we reach 840 or close to it:\n\nAssume \\( n = 4 \\):\n\\[\n(n-1)n(n+2)(n+1) = 4 \\times 5 \\times 6 \\times 7 = 840\n\\]\n\nThis works! Therefore, the integers are \\( n-1, n, n+1, n+2 \\which are 3, 4, 5, 6 \\).\n\nThus, the largest of the four integers is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
